{
  "_": "Agent harness rules for this repo. Read by ~/.claude/loops/*, whose source lives in harness/loops/ HERE — so this file doubles as the worked example the harness README describes. Everything is optional; omitted keys fall back to harness_rules.py DEFAULTS, which are the safe end of every switch. path/github/default_branch are NOT settable — they are detected from the checkout.",
  "_migration": "This file was TRACKED as `.harness-rules` until it split in two. The tracked half is policy and lives here; the untracked half holds only what one MACHINE's providers will actually serve (reviewers.<seat>.{enabled,model,effort}, and narrowing only) — see harness_rules.py. That half is read from TWO places and the repo's wins per key: `$QUARTERBACK_HARNESS_RULES` or `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/quarterback/harness-rules.json` for the whole box (prefer this — it is read by every repo and every worktree on the machine, so a fresh worktree is correct the moment it exists), and `.harness-rules` beside this file for this checkout alone. Migrating a repo is one commit: `git mv .harness-rules .harness-rules.sample`. Two things to know. (1) If YOUR checkout had local edits to the old tracked file, copy it aside before pulling that commit — the rename deletes the tracked path, and the copy is what becomes your per-box overlay, which is gitignored from then on. (2) If a repo does the two halves in two commits, both files end up tracked: that is safe, the sample wins as the baseline and the resolver names the file it did not read, and `git rm --cached .harness-rules` finishes it (untracking the file while keeping it on disk, where it is now the overlay).",
  "_two_refs": "Unattended runs (the systemd timer) read this file from `origin/<default branch>`, never from the working tree, so a poisoned PR cannot rewrite the rules governing its own review. The untracked overlay is part of the working tree whoever put it there — code run from a PR checkout can create it — so it is read on the INTERACTIVE path only.",

  "mode": {
    "name": "cleanroom",
    "_": "How this repo is worked (#178). The unit of work is a GitHub issue: claim it, take your OWN worktree with `create-worktree`, land through a PR. The other mode is `jungle` — shared checkout, commits straight to the branch, structure carried on the board instead of in issues and PRs — which is how 65lowther is worked and is not a lesser way to work.",
    "_declaring_it_is_not_a_no_op": "`cleanroom` is also the built-in default, so it would be easy to read this key as decoration. It is not. `qb-mode` distinguishes a repo that DECLARED cleanroom from one that merely inherited it, and warns on thinner evidence about the first: being in the primary checkout is enough on its own. An undeclared repo is given the benefit of the doubt until the checkout is one that actually hands out worktrees, because it might be somebody's private clone that no second agent will ever open. Writing this line is what turns the guard on for this repo.",
    "_why_it_is_stated_here": "This repo has broken its own rule twice with nothing to stop it. 2026-08-17: three agents in one checkout, and a `nix build` compiled one agent's in-progress edits as another's evidence — caught only by diffing two derivations. 2026-08-25: four agents in the same checkout, and a `git reset --hard` typed by one destroyed another's uncommitted review fixes. Neither time did anyone CHOOSE to work in the shared tree; the session simply started there and nothing said this repo is not worked that way. `qb-mode` exits 3 in this checkout now, and the session-start hook says so before any work begins. The mode is read from `origin/<default branch>` and never from the working tree, so an edit in front of you — uncommitted, or on a branch that has not landed — cannot turn the warning off; policy for a shared checkout is not something one branch decides for everybody standing in it.",
    "_axes": "`isolation` (worktree|shared) and `landing` (pr|direct) are settable on their own and both are omitted here, which takes them from the preset. They exist because the two dials are independent even though they move together today: a repo that wanted its own worktrees but direct commits would say \"landing\": \"direct\" and still be a cleanroom tree."
  },

  "reviewers": {
    "_": "claude stays on the default `opus`, a floating alias that always resolves to the current model and so cannot rot. The other three are pinned, because their slugs are versioned build names.",

    "codex": {
      "model": "gpt-5.6-luna",
      "effort": "max",
      "_": "Pinned rather than left on the CLI's own default, which is whatever that install happens to ship and therefore differs between boxes — the panel is a controlled comparison, and an unpinned seat makes 'codex found more than claude' unattributable to a model.",
      "_deliberate_override": "This pin argues WITH the DEFAULTS comment in harness_rules.py, which declines to pin codex globally on the grounds that a slug is a versioned build name that gets retired and takes a whole vendor with it. That reasoning is right for the fleet default and is overridden here on purpose: this repo IS the panel's test bench, so an unattributable comparison costs it more than a rotted pin, and the pin's failure is loud (see _precondition) rather than a silently thinner review. The liability is real and per-box — a machine on codex < 0.144 loses the seat entirely until the pin is cleared — so the answer when that happens is to bump the slug or drop this key, not to wonder why codex went quiet. Verified on one machine on one date; treat it as a claim about that box, not about the fleet.",
      "_effort": "luna accepts low|medium|high|xhigh|max but NOT ultra, so `max` is the top of its range. Spelled `model_reasoning_effort` via `-c` on this CLI, `--thinking` on pi — same config key here, different word there.",
      "_precondition": "Needs codex >= 0.144; an older CLI is refused by the API with a 400 'requires a newer version of Codex'. That failure is loud rather than silent — `is_rejection` short-circuits the retries and the report names the model that was refused — so a rotted pin costs a reviewer visibly. Verified 2026-08-15 on codex-cli 0.147.0: the exact panel argv (`codex exec … --model gpt-5.6-luna -c model_reasoning_effort=max`) returned a well-formed findings array at exit 0."
    },

    "antigravity": {
      "enabled": true,
      "model": "gemini-3.7-flash-high",
      "_": "Third vendor. The DEFAULTS keep this seat off, because `agy` is a workstation package authenticating against a personal Google account and so never reaches a headless box; it is on HERE because this repo is developed on those workstations and a third vendor is worth having. Host scope is enforced by where the package lives, not by this switch: panel.py skips any reviewer whose CLI is not on PATH, so a headless box records `antigravity: CLI absent` and runs the panel without it.",
      "_headless_cost": "That skip used to cost more than a seat. `coverage_veto` turned every reviewer with `ran: False` into a veto line, and `round_stop` computes `confident` as `not veto`, so two permanently-absent seats made a confident stop unreachable on exactly the boxes that run unattended — a real signal converted into constant noise. Fixed in panel.py rather than by scoping this switch: a reviewer whose CLI is not installed is a fact about the HOST, not about the round, so it is reported without vetoing. That absence is recorded as state on the reviewer's run (`ReviewerRun.absent`), not inferred from the wording of the skip line. Every other way of not running still vetoes, and so does a round in which NO reviewer ran at all.",
      "_model": "Pinned to a slug `agy models` actually lists, and confirmed by RUNNING it — 2026-08-15, the exact panel argv (`agy --mode plan -p … --model gemini-3.7-flash-high`) returned a well-formed findings array at exit 0. Name resolution, model availability and remaining quota are three different questions, and only a real run answers the last two.",
      "_argv_ceiling": "This seat has a SECOND standing cost, and it is exempted for the same reason as the first (#113). `agy` is the only reviewer whose prompt travels in argv \u2014 it cannot read one off stdin \u2014 so the kernel caps what it can be handed at ARGV_PROMPT_MAX_BYTES, and on a large diff it structurally cannot see all of it: measured on PR #160, 116,771 of 175,547 chars, 66.5%. That is a fact about this BOX and this diff size, true every round, so `coverage_veto` reports the truncation without spending the round's confidence on it. Recorded as state (`reviewers.antigravity.argv_capped` in the payload), and it takes BOTH halves: `argv_clamp` says the kernel is what cut the budget, and the composed material says the seat actually lost some of the review target. Truncation by a `max_diff_chars` this box could have carried is a number somebody typed, is fixable, and still vetoes \u2014 and so does a clamp that shrinks the prompt to nothing, because a seat handed no diff reviewed nothing. The floor: if every seat that ran was cut this way, the veto stands.",
      "_no_effort": "`effort` is deliberately unset. agy bakes the reasoning level into the slug — `agy models` lists gemini-3.7-flash-high / -medium / -low as three separate models — so an `--effort` on top would be a second, conflicting knob for the same thing.",
      "_not_gemini_cli": "This is `agy` (Antigravity), NOT gemini-cli: a different CLI on a different endpoint. lexray dropped `gemini-3.7-flash` from its gemini-cli seat because every call 429'd on an exhausted free-tier bucket shared with 3.5. That finding does not transfer to this seat, and the control run above is what says so rather than the assumption."
    },

    "pi": {
      "enabled": true,
      "model": "openrouter/moonshotai/kimi-k3",
      "effort": "high",
      "_": "Fourth vendor, off in the DEFAULTS for the same host reason as antigravity and on here for the same repo reason. It is the only member reaching outside the three US frontier labs — claude, codex and antigravity are trained similarly enough to agree for the same reasons, so Kimi is the one seat that can disagree for a different one. That is the whole point of a diverse panel.",
      "_model": "Fully qualified because pi fronts many providers and openrouter is the only one authenticated here (`pi auth check --provider openrouter` -> ready, 2026-08-15). A bare slug would be sent to an unauthenticated default. Verified with the exact panel argv, same run as above.",
      "_effort": "Maps to pi's `--thinking`, whose set (off|minimal|low|medium|high|xhigh|max) is NOT codex's — see EFFORTS in panel.py. Same config key, different word on each CLI.",
      "_cost": "The only METERED member: the other three ride subscriptions, this one bills per token through OpenRouter. It is the seat to look at first if the OpenRouter bill surprises you.",
      "_blast_radius": "Stated because a committed `enabled: true` on a metered seat is a standing charge, not a per-invocation choice. It bills on EVERY panel run of this repo on a box that carries `pi` — including the unattended lander and epic loops, and once per round of a multi-round fix cycle, which re-runs the whole panel each round. That is the intended scope: this repo is the panel's test bench and a fourth vendor outside the US frontier labs is what it is for. The brake for a run that should not pay it is `panel.py --reviewers claude,codex`, which overrides this file for that run; the brake for a rate that stops being worth it is flipping this key.",
      "_hosts": "Same story as antigravity — `pi` is a workstation package, and a box without it records `pi: CLI absent` rather than failing the panel."
    },

    "sonarqube": {
      "enabled": false,
      "_": "No SonarCloud project covers this repo, so the panel runs with no hard gate and every finding is a soft one judged on its merits. Stated explicitly rather than omitted: the default is off too, but a reader of this file should not have to guess whether the absence was a decision or an oversight."
    }
  },

  "review_panel": {
    "reviewer_code_access": true,
    "_reviewer_code_access": "Left at the default, and stated anyway, because this is the one governance key whose right value depends on WHO opens the PRs. On, because this repo's contributors are the fleet's own agents \u2014 they already hold a shell on this box as this user, so a hostile AGENTS.md smuggled into a reviewer's cwd is not a door any of them needs. The convention-file strip is a DENYLIST and will rot as vendors add files; that is the accepted cost here, and it is exactly why a repo taking outside contributions sets this false instead. #52's epic, which points the panel at PRs that are not ours, is the first caller expected to want it off.",
    "_reviewer_code_access_reach": "It buys ONE seat today. Only claude can be told 'read but do not execute' (--allowedTools Read Grep Glob, no Bash) and it enforces a working-directory boundary of its own; codex's -c knobs only REMOVE tools and its single read path is the shell, so granting reads grants execution (against #92) and re-opens the tool-hunt that once spent 99% of a run; pi's --no-tools is all-or-nothing over read/bash/edit/write; antigravity has no tool mechanism at all. So codex, pi and antigravity keep the empty sandbox, and their could_not_assess entries stay exempt from the coverage veto while claude's count again. That split is recorded per round in the payload (code_access.seats) \u2014 a seat that can read the tree while another cannot is a bigger confound than an unpinned model.",
    "fix_severity_floor": "P4",
    "round_trigger_floor": "P2",
    "low_severity_fix_lines": 40,
    "low_severity_fix_full_chars": 14325,
    "max_fix_growth": 3.0,
    "max_fix_growth_chars": 30000,
    "min_fix_growth_chars": 2000,
    "max_fix_guard_lines": null,
    "threshold_by_severity": {},
    "reviewer_scope": "diff",
    "fixer_may_defer": true,
    "file_deferral_issues": "shape",
    "require_failing_test": false,
    "max_rounds": 6,
    "escalate_on": {"premise_repeated": 2, "fix_injection": 0.5},
    "_78_threshold": "threshold_by_severity (#78) is how many DISTINCT members must independently raise a finding at a given severity before it is this round's work. {\\\"P3\\\": 2} reads \\\"a P3 one seat raised is reported, not fixed\\\". A band the mapping does not name needs one seat, which is what every band needed before this key existed, so {} is the whole off switch and is what is written here.\\n\\nIT IS THE REVIEWER-SIDE HALF OF THE CONVERGENCE PROBLEM. Every other brake in this block acts on a finding already accepted as work: low_severity_fix_lines bounds what a pass may spend on it, unrefereed_line_weight prices where the spending lands, max_fix_guard_lines bounds what one pass may write, and #616's rule makes a fixer name who consumes the code before changing it. This one acts before all of them and asks the question none of them can \u2014 should this have become work at all?\\n\\nTHE EVIDENCE, AND WHY IT IS NOT ENOUGH TO SHIP ARMED. #78's table, 2026-08-20: of eight findings adjudicated by hand, the four refuted (32-F06, 64-F02, 64-F03, 64-F04) were each raised by ONE seat and every multi-seat finding was real. Two things in the same table say a count cannot be trusted with the decision: 32-F01 was solo and real, and #64's round 1 was a panel of ONE (#68), where every finding is solo by construction and a threshold of 2 discards the round entire. And judge confirmation demonstrably does not filter these \u2014 on lexray#1780 round 3 a P2 was raised by a seat AND confirmed by the master judge and was still wrong, and complying with it introduced the entitlement leak round 4 then found. So this ships {} on max_fix_guard_lines' precedent: eight findings on two pull requests is an observation, and #67's rule is that an instrument earns a gate over a few dozen cycles or not at all. The instrument is already published \u2014 `reviewers` on every finding in the payload, and the star-consensus notation the report has carried since #62 \u2014 so measure this repo's own precision by seat count before writing a number.\\n\\nIT IS THE ONE DIAL IN THIS BLOCK THAT CAN HIDE A REAL DEFECT, so it is bounded in CODE rather than by the default. panel_seats.Dials.corroboration_applies refuses to apply a threshold to any severity at or above round_trigger_floor, or to P1/P2 at any floor (panel_core.BLOCKING_SEVERITIES, which is round_stop rule 2's own bar). A repo writing {\\\"P1\\\": 2} gets a round that applies 1 and a config_notes line saying the key was ignored. Two consequences, and both are the point: a single seat finding a genuine P1 is handed to the fixer exactly as it always was, and anything a threshold CAN stand down is a finding round_stop's rules 1, 2 and 3 all ignore already \u2014 so a stood-down finding cannot hold the cycle open and round_stop needed no new parameter.\\n\\nREPORTED, NEVER SUPPRESSED. A finding under its band's threshold keeps its master verdict, keeps its board row, and is printed under its own heading with the seat count that stood it down \u2014 the disposal a below-floor finding already gets (#165), for the same reason: a finding that vanishes is a finding nobody can argue with. It carries below_threshold and seats_required in the payload so an orchestrator building a fixer's brief can see the suppression rather than reconstruct it. 1 at a band is legal and is the identity; 0 is refused, because no finding is raised by fewer than one seat and a hand that wrote it meant either 1 or the off switch.",
    "_618_guard_delta": "max_fix_guard_lines (#618) is the guard half of the growth ceiling, and the only one measured per FIX PASS: test and prose lines ONE pass may churn. null here, which is the shipped default and is UNSET rather than off — there is no calibration to write. THE MEASUREMENT, on lexray#1780, five rounds: guard_ratio read 2.21 -> 2.19 -> 2.13 -> 2.09 -> 2.02 while source went 476 -> 941 and test went 883 -> 1,632. It FELL MONOTONICALLY THROUGH THE RUNAWAY IT WAS WATCHING, because a proportion cannot tell a well-guarded change from a change whose guards are running away with it — the runaway moves both halves together. A max_guard_ratio would have fired on none of those five rounds and would fire at round 1 on a heavily guarded PR that never churned: wrong in both directions, which is why there is no such key (harness_rules.py carries that decision in full). What the per-round DELTA sees is the event itself: rounds 2-5 wrote 380, 205, 205 and 58 lines of test and prose against 177, 116, 114 and 58 of production.\n\nWHY IT IS STILL NULL. That is one cycle. Its quiet round wrote 58 guard lines and its loud one 380, and a threshold drawn between them is a number chosen to fit one PR with its argument written afterwards — #67's instrument-before-gate rule refusing a ceiling nobody has earned. So the count is TAKEN and PUBLISHED every round (round_stop.guard_churn, and the prod/test/prose columns in the round table) and nothing fires. Write a number here when a few dozen cycles have said what one is.\n\nIT IS A CEILING ON A PASS AND IT DOES NOT BANK: each round reads the churn of its own fix range and nothing earlier, so a quiet round cannot fund a loud one. Same mechanical counting as low_severity_fix_lines — panel_seats.referee_split over the range's own diff, never a forecast and never the fixer's judgement about its own work. 0 is refused (every healthy pass carrying a regression test would cross it); null is the off switch.",
    "_618_guard_lines_action": "escalate_on.guard_lines decides what CROSSING that ceiling does, and it is false here and in the DEFAULTS. Two keys because #67 wants the weaker action first on an instrument with one cycle behind it: a repo that writes a ceiling gets it measured and REPORTED, and has to arm this flag before a round ends on it. max_fix_growth ends a cycle on #188 and #236; this has lexray#1780 and nothing else. Armed, it is round_stop's sixth rung and takes the same two bounds as the four beside it — it can only turn a `go again` into a STOP, and only the round rule 1 was buying, so it cannot cancel the repair round for a P1 an earlier round raised. Arming it with max_fix_guard_lines null is a rung that can never fire, and the round says so in config_notes rather than leaving it to be discovered.",
    "budget": {"tokens_per_day": null, "runs_per_day": null, "tokens_per_round": null, "tokens_per_pr": null, "runs_per_pr": null, "fleet_tokens_per_day": null},
    "budget_window_hours": 24,
    "distant_merge_lines": 20,
    "local_suite": ["uv run --extra dev pytest harness/loops/tests -q -n auto"],
    "local_suite_timeout": 600,
    "_548_local_suite": "#548's channel filler, and the one key in this file that names something to EXECUTE. When GitHub reports `none` or `unknown` for a commit, the panel runs this before dispatching the seats and hands them the answer through `ci_brief` in three states of its own \u2014 a local pass earns the round its confident stop and buys no merge, because `preland.check_ci` reads GitHub and has never heard of it. (`blocked` is deliberately NOT in that set: a gated run is actionable, and hiding the click behind a local pass is not a floor under the seats.) It runs only where this checkout is already sitting on the PR's head with nothing uncommitted or unignored in it, which is the fix loop's own worktree and nothing else.\n\nREAD FROM origin/main, NOT FROM THIS FILE IN YOUR WORKING TREE. Every other key here resolves from the branch in front of you on the interactive path; this one does not, because a panel round usually runs from a worktree checked out at the PR's own head \u2014 so the working tree's copy of this file is the pull request's, and a command read from it would be one the PR chose, run by the thing reviewing it. The practical consequence: editing this line does nothing until the edit lands on main, including for the PR that edits it.\n\nWHY ONE SUITE AND NOT THE WHOLE OF IT. `tests/` needs a live Postgres and the app's environment; `harness/loops/tests` needs neither, runs in about two minutes under `-n auto`, and is the suite the panel itself is made of \u2014 so it is the run whose green actually refutes 'this new test never runs' for the diffs a round here is most often reading. It names that one suite rather than `pytest harness` because `pytest harness` is not green from the repo root and never has been: it collects `harness/templates/test_migrations_self_contained.py`, which CI's own loop (`find harness -type d -name tests`) does not run at all, and measured on 2026-08-28 it failed alongside three `test_qb_dash` click-offset tests for reasons that have nothing to do with any diff. A local suite that is red for standing reasons would report `local-fail` on every round, which is a veto that carries no information \u2014 the failure mode this whole feature is against. A repo whose suite needs a service names the DB-backed target as a second entry in this list on the boxes that have one; the list runs in order and stops at the first failure.\n\nHOW OFTEN IT WILL ACTUALLY FIRE HERE: rarely, and that is honest rather than disappointing. `.github/workflows/tests.yml` runs on every pull request, so this repo normally has a real CI answer, and a real answer is never displaced by a weaker local one. What is left is a `gh` outage (`unknown`) and a commit no run was ever created for (`none`). It is written out anyway for the reason the dials above it are: this repo is the panel's test bench, and a setting nobody has ever resolved from a real config is a setting whose first real use is its first test.\n\n600 rather than the 900 default, because that is a bound on THIS suite: it takes about two minutes, so a run still going at ten is wedged rather than slow, and a bound set far above what the thing takes only fires long after it stopped being useful.",
    "_278_distant_merge_lines": "#278's dial, at its DEFAULTS value and written out anyway like the eight above. After an integration merge the reviewer asks how much of the merge is genuinely new material to THIS PR: git diff between the commit the round read and the merge result, restricted to the files this PR touches, counted in changed lines. At or under 20 the merge is DISTANT and the round that ran before it still stands (nothing is claimed as reviewed that was not \u2014 the merged code is not this PR's change); past it the merge is INVOLVED and its resolution is unreviewed work that gets reviewed, only it and not the whole PR again. Lines rather than file overlap or hunk overlap: file overlap is a hair-trigger that would send every base merge back to a full re-review, hunk overlap cannot be read the same way from GitHub's compare API and from a local git diff so the two ends of the feature would disagree, and lines is continuous \u2014 which is what makes a dial mean something rather than rename a boolean. 20 and at the LOW end deliberately: reading INVOLVED when the merge was distant buys one scoped round, while reading DISTANT when it was involved ships a hand-resolved merge nobody read, which is #80's stderr_gist incident \u2014 a landed fix silently reverted because a function that had MOVED met a main that already had it, git conflicting on neither. This repo is where that happened, so it runs the shipped default. null is the off switch (every head move is a review of earlier code, the pre-#278 behaviour); 0 admits only a resolution that is empty over this PR's own files. A range with no merge commit in it is never distant whatever its size \u2014 that is a push, and unreviewed work of this PR's own kind holds at any size.",
    "_165": "The seven dials from #165, every one of them left at its DEFAULTS value and written out anyway \u2014 this repo IS the panel's test bench and the measurement that set these numbers was taken here, so a reader of this file should not have to open harness_rules.py to find out what policy the panel ran under. That measurement, across the seven PRs panelled on 2026-08-16: the last round of each raised 201 findings no earlier round had, 128 of them (63.7%) created by the fix pass immediately before it, against a ~7% industry baseline for bad-fix injection. All seven panels ended on the round cap. Nine of this repo's open issues are the panel's own deferred-finding overflow.",
    "_165_floors": "fix_severity_floor and round_trigger_floor are two keys because they are two questions. As of 2026-08-30 this repo answers them P4/P2, which is the shipped DEFAULTS split, and the P4 half is Rich's rule taken on #621: \"we should fix what needs fixing (P1s and P2s) ... [no] budget for anything that would block us closing, and limited budget for things that wouldn't block us closing (generally P3 and P4) — that's the point, we try to pick them up, but don't want to let it cause a ballooning of issues.\"\n\nWHAT LOWERING THE FIX FLOOR TO P4 ACTUALLY DOES, because \"lower the floor\" reads as \"fix more\" and that is not the mechanism. What BLOCKS is round_trigger_floor, which stays at P2 and is unbudgeted by decision (#614). The fix floor only says how far DOWN a pass may reach while it is already open, and low_severity_fix_lines — 40 churned lines, priced by unrefereed_line_weight — is the whole of what it may spend down there. The band was the P3s; it is now the P3s and the P4s, spent cheapest-first by a count rather than by the fixer's judgement. So this admits P4 to a BUDGET, not to an obligation.\n\nTHE RISK, AND IT IS THE BUDGET'S TO CARRY. A lower floor is a wider licence to touch. P4 was 31.3% of findings in #165's measurement and is the tier that ballooned PR #236 — a 54-line README rewrite and a decode-path rework, both P4 — and that argument has not been refuted, it has been priced. With the floor at the bottom band there is no tier left below it, so nothing but the budget limits the reach: at low_severity_fix_lines: null this pair reads \"fix everything\", which is the pre-#165 behaviour the convergence work exists to undo. If the P3/P4 tier starts accumulating again, move the budget first and this key second (#297, and _297_budget below).\n\nTHE HISTORY, kept because this key has now moved in three directions on evidence. BOTH WERE P2 on 2026-08-20, and it was right at the time: measured across the four PRs then open, 132 master-confirmed findings, 5 P1 and 32 P2, and a P2 floor dropped 95 of 132 (72.0%) losing no P1. Every one of those four PRs had ended on the round cap with its findings handed to nobody (#42), so the P3 tier was not being fixed in the round already open — it was being deferred into an issue nobody would action: 15 such issues, ~290 findings. P3 CAME BACK on 2026-08-22, once low_severity_fix_lines (#297) and a fixed max_fix_growth (#298) meant the tier could be reached without the accumulation that made it unaffordable — #188's 185-line feature had become 721 churned lines, 74% of it review-response code, off a round-2 list that was 89% below P2.\n\nTHE WAY BACK IS ONE KEY. P3 restores 2026-08-22, P2 restores the measured cut (across the seven PRs panelled on 2026-08-16, a P2 cut discards 99 of 147 findings and loses ZERO P1s, all six in the kept tier), and both arguments are above rather than deleted.",
    "_482_deferral_issues": "#482's dial, at its DEFAULTS value, and as of 2026-08-30 it is not a severity cut at all: it is a SHAPE rule. Which deferrals get a GitHub ISSUE as well as the board row every deferral gets anyway. Rich's decision on #621, filed as #620: \"we should allow category and true important issues with complexity to be deferred, as single items, but not 'here are 20 P3 and P4s' which is just transferring a problem to me.\"\n\nSO THE GATE ASKS WHAT SHAPE, NOT HOW SEVERE. A CATEGORY — one standing item for a recurring class, which is #620's own proposal 1 and the form a human can work as a batch — gets an issue. A SINGLE NAMED ITEM with real substance behind it, one defect or one decision owed or one piece of complexity somebody has to sit down with, gets an issue whatever severity it carries. A BATCH — a round's leftovers swept into one ticket — gets board rows and never an issue, whatever its severity mix, a P1 in the pile included: a batch is not a work item, it is a round's remainder wearing one, and the P1 inside it is better served by the row that chains across rounds and gets read than by being the fourth bullet of a dump.\n\nMEASURED HERE, 2026-08-26 and re-counted under the panel/deferred-findings label on 2026-08-30. Twenty open issues are the panel's own deferred-finding exhaust and nothing else: #66 #69 #72 #74 #95 #104 #111 #119 #120 #126 #132 #133 #140 #223 #237 #283 #285 #286 #288 #300. Created between 2026-08-15 and 2026-08-21, carrying 345 findings by their own titles, and NOT ONE HAS EVER BEEN CLOSED — zero drain in fifteen days. #283 is a rescue FROM one of them: three live defects that had been sitting inside a deferred-findings dump nobody read. Rich, on the day of the measurement: \"the board itself should hold the dumping ground of fiddly P3 and P4 issues we have left lying around.\" AND EVERY ONE OF THE TWENTY IS A BATCH, which is what the severity cut could not see: severity is a property of a finding, batchness is a property of the ticket, so a cut anywhere on P1..P4 files some batches and blocks some single items — exactly backwards.\n\nTHIS KNOWINGLY AMENDS #42's REMEDY, and #42's rule still stands: a capped round's findings must be handed to somebody and must not evaporate. What changes is the DESTINATION for a batch — the board rather than GitHub — and nothing else. Below the gate the finding still gets its deferred row, still carries the one-line note saying what it is and why it was not fixed, and is still relayed to the human; what it does not get is a second copy on a backlog with no drain. deferred_to is nullable, the API accepts a deferred outcome without one, and /panel renders a targetless row rather than treating it as broken. That makes the board the ONLY record a batch has, which is why the read side matters: GET /review/findings?repo=&pr= returns each chain with its outcome, and #508 is the cross-PR query this must not foreclose.\n\nTHE WAY BACK IS ONE KEY and the old vocabulary is kept: P2 restores the severity cut exactly as it ran from #482 until 2026-08-30, any of P1..P4 states that cut at another band, always restores the pre-#482 behaviour of an issue per deferral, never files none at all. An escalation is exempt at EVERY setting — its issue asks a question rather than filing a task, and it is what carries that question past the end of the session.",
    "_297_budget": "low_severity_fix_lines (#297) is the churned lines a round may spend on findings between fix_severity_floor and round_trigger_floor — at P4/P2 since 2026-08-30, the P3s and the P4s together. Spent cheapest-first, counted with git diff --numstat after the fact rather than forecast before it, and the remainder is reported and recorded rather than dropped.\n\nIT IS NOW THE ONLY CONTROL ON THE REACH OF A FIX PASS, which is what changed on 2026-08-30. It shipped inert on 2026-08-21 (both floors at P2, empty band), came alive on 2026-08-22 when the fix floor went back to P3, and now covers a band with no tier below it. 40 is still the shipped default and has never been exercised here; if the P3/P4 tier starts accumulating, this is the first number to move, before the floor.\n\nWHAT IS ON THIS BUDGET AND WHAT IS NOT — the sentence 848 lines walked past (#618). The blocking band is unbudgeted by decision (#614), and unbudgeted is not unaccounted. THE ASSERTION THAT DEMONSTRATES A FIX IS PART OF THAT FIX, at any severity: the test that goes red without the change and green with it is the fix's own evidence (#114 requires it) and is never charged here. ADDITIONAL test work is not — a neighbouring assertion strengthened, coverage the finding did not ask for, prose nobody's finding named — that is an OBSERVATION, it is charged to this budget at unrefereed_line_weight, and where the budget will not pay for it, it is recorded rather than written. Without that line drawn, \"the blocking band is unbudgeted\" is a licence: on lexray#1780 the fix passes after round 1 wrote 1,313 lines of which 848 were test and doc, nearly all of it under a blocking finding's cover where no budget could see it.\n\nA budget rather than a per-fix cap because #188's round 1 was not one balloon — it was 408 lines of individually reasonable small fixes, which no per-fix cap can see.",
    "_551_budget_size": "low_severity_fix_full_chars (#551) is the PROPORTIONAL half of that budget, at its DEFAULTS value, and it is written as a SIZE rather than a rate: at or above this many chars of the cycle's FIRST round the whole low_severity_fix_lines budget applies, and below it the budget is pro rata \u2014 lines x first_chars / this. The round spends whichever half is smaller, so the pair can only ever tighten.\n\nIT IS THE OPPOSITE OPERATOR TO #664's FLOOR AND THE SYMMETRY ARGUMENT GETS IT BACKWARDS. max_fix_growth's defect is that a proportional allowance shrinks with the PR while diff framing does not, so a tiny PR cannot afford one honest fix \u2014 that wants a floor. What THIS budget measures (#297) is accumulation RELATIVE TO THE CHANGE: PR #188's feature became 721 churned lines, 74% of it review-response code. Its dangerous end is therefore the SMALL PR, where a fixed 40 lines can exceed the diff it is polishing, while on a 5,000-line PR 40 lines of P3 work is harmless noise. Anyone implementing this from \"40 lines is noise on a big PR\" writes a max, and that is the version that reintroduces #188.\n\nCHARS, AND THAT IS A CORRECTNESS POINT RATHER THAN A STYLE ONE. The budget is counted in churned lines and the only first-round size a baseline records is pr_chars, so the companion had to be written in one unit or the other. In chars it states its crossing in the unit the code actually compares and needs no chars-per-line rate AT RUN TIME \u2014 the arithmetic is a ratio of two char counts multiplying a line count, so the units cancel. An earlier draft was a percentage over a CHARS_PER_CHURNED_LINE constant; both are gone.\n\n14,325 IS MEASURED IN CHARS RATHER THAN CONVERTED INTO THEM. #551's policy is a ratio \u2014 \"on a 179-line PR, 40 lines is a sane ~22% allowance\" \u2014 which puts the full budget at about 40 / 0.22 = 182 churned lines. Rather than multiplying that by an assumed rate, every merged PR of the quarterback repo whose churn is between 120 and 260 lines (n=21) was fetched, measured in chars and scaled to exactly 182 churned lines: median 14,325, range 9,538 to 18,604. It is the size PRs of that shape actually are, it is re-runnable, and the spread is stated rather than hidden.\n\nWHAT THAT IS AND IS NOT FREE OF, NARROWLY. The run-time arithmetic is rate-free and that part is unqualified. The calibration is not: it anchors on 182 CHURNED LINES and scales each of the 21 PRs to that count, which needs each PR's own chars-per-line, so the line framing moved out of the formula and into the number. What the number IS free of is the disputed 66-versus-58 rate, because every PR contributed its own measured chars and its own measured churn rather than one assumed rate standing in for all of them. What it still rests on is churn being counted the same way across this repo \u2014 which is exactly what #692 is open about, PR #188 carrying three different line counts (521, 597, 721) in three places. Less exposed, not immune. In the unit the dial is written in: 40,000 chars gets the full 40 lines, 10,000 gets 27, 5,000 gets 13, 2,000 gets 5.\n\nTHE BUDGET IS CLAMPED AT ONE HONEST ONE-LINE FIX. Pro rata alone reaches 1 line at 359 chars and 3 at 1,075, and a budget of one or two lines is not a small budget \u2014 it is \"fix nothing\" written so it reads like a budget. git diff --numstat reports AGGREGATE insertions and deletions per file and has no notion of a changed line; what is true is that a one-line replacement commonly arrives as one deletion plus one insertion (verified), and _referee_kind_lines counts every - and + alike. So the cheapest correction to an existing line commonly costs 2, and the commonest below-floor fix is a comment or stale docstring, which unrefereed_line_weight prices at 2 apiece. The clamp is therefore 2 x unrefereed_line_weight \u2014 4 at the shipped weight \u2014 and it moves with the weight because the weight is the unit the budget is counted in. SINCE #674 A STARVED BUDGET IS EXPENSIVE RATHER THAN UNTIDY: an unpayable fix is declared --declined <key>:budget, that declaration appends a veto, the veto costs the round its stop_confident, and preland --require-earned-stop turns a false stop_confident into a failed check \u2014 so a budget too small to buy anything holds the PR out of a strict landing for the rest of the cycle. Below 1,791 chars the clamp IS the budget \u2014 simply where the pro-rata share first rises above it, at 5, and NOT where a second such fix becomes affordable, which is 2,865. A concession, made in the direction #674 prices.\n\nA FIRST ROUND MEASURED AT ZERO CHARS GETS THE CLAMP AND NOT THE WHOLE BUDGET. An unknown first-round size restores the absolute, because the denominator is genuinely absent and a guessed one is worse than none; a measured zero is a different claim \u2014 something was read and it was nothing \u2014 and the smallest measurement there is must not buy the largest budget there is. It needs no special case: written x 0 // size is 0, which the clamp lifts.\n\nIT CAN NEVER RAISE A BUDGET. The clamp is applied INSIDE min(written, ...), so a repo at low_severity_fix_lines: 3 gets 3 and not 4, and a written 0 \u2014 which raises the applied floor to the trigger cut \u2014 is left exactly where it is. null is the proportional half off and the exact pre-#551 behaviour; a size written beside low_severity_fix_lines: null bounds nothing and gets a config_notes line rather than silently becoming a budget. 0 is refused, because a size every PR is at or above is the off position written in a spelling that does not say so.\n\nTHE SHAPE IS A HUMAN'S DECISION AND #551 IS LABELLED THAT WAY. What was rejected: a two-part low_severity_fix_lines (BOARD_DIALS types each dial as a scalar number and null already means off, so a pair would have to say which half a bare null switches off \u2014 on a key where null and 0 already differ); a percentage with a chars-per-line constant, which stated its crossing in a unit the code does not compare and inherited #692; a churned-line count on the baseline, which needs no size at all and declines to run on every cycle in flight, the objection #492 used against a _lines dial; and any change to cheapest-first ordering, which #551 rules out because a value term hands back the judgement #297 removed.",
    "_165_growth": "max_fix_growth is the backstop for the case the floors cannot reach: a fix pass that has ALREADY written a second change. On PR #236 three rounds went 18 -> 25 -> 24 findings with 19 of round 3's 24 attributed by the panel to the preceding fix pass, the PR grew from 359 to 2,313 insertions, none of the 67 findings was in the bug fix it existed for, and the last fix pass introduced an unbounded hang (read_bytes() on a FIFO, with the guard against exactly that dropped). It landed at 62 insertions. 3.0 is deliberately loose \u2014 a genuine fix that adds the tests the review asked for can double a small diff, and that is not this shape. `null` here is the off switch, which is the one place in this file where null does not mean 'inherit'.",
    "_492_growth_chars": "max_fix_growth_chars is the ABSOLUTE half of that ceiling (#492), at its DEFAULTS value and written out like the dials above it. The multiple alone hands its rope out in proportion to the starting size: at 3.0x a 113-line PR may grow ~226 lines and a 2,000-line one may grow 4,000, so the loosest allowance goes to the case most in need of a ceiling. The cycle stops on whichever of the two is crossed FIRST, which means the PAIR OF CEILINGS can only ever tighten — nothing that arrangement lets through would have been caught by the multiple alone. That is a claim about these two keys and NOT about the growth check as a whole: min_fix_growth_chars (#664) is a floor under the multiple and is the one term here that loosens, so the sentence was narrowed in the commit that added it rather than left to be discovered.\n\nCHARS AND NOT LINES, and the unit is in the name. max_fix_growth divides pr_chars by pr_chars, and two halves of one ceiling read off two different measurements is #298's defect one level up. 30,000 is roughly 380-450 churned lines at PR #188's own measured 66 chars a churned line; it stops #188's growth of 536 lines and #236's of 1,954, with margin, and does NOT stop the 113-line cycle in #492, which grew ~122 lines — correctly, since that is the 'binds a round late' half of the report and guard_ratio is the earlier signal filed for it.\n\nA SECOND KEY rather than a two-part max_fix_growth value: BOARD_DIALS types this dial as a scalar number, the board stores one JSON value per dial, and null is already the off switch for max_fix_growth — a pair would have to answer which half a bare null switched off. Two keys, two nulls, and null on BOTH is the pre-#165 behaviour of no growth check at all. A repo that wrote max_fix_growth: null before this landed is told in config_notes that the other half is still in force.\n\nGUARD_RATIO STAYS REPORT-ONLY, PERMANENTLY — Rich's decision of 2026-08-30, answering #618's second question, and it is a decision rather than another deferral. #67 asked for the instrument before the gate; the instrument has run and there is no gate to build. A ratio of test lines to source lines is not a demonstrated observable failure, and only a demonstrated observable failure may block (#623). The measurement says the same from the other side: on lexray#1780 the ratio read 2.21 → 2.19 → 2.13 → 2.09 → 2.02 over five rounds while both of its halves nearly doubled, so it fell monotonically through the runaway it was watching — a proportion cannot tell \"well guarded\" from \"the change and its guards are both running away\". There is no max_guard_ratio key and there will not be one; the column is for a human reading it beside the churn counts, which is what #618's third part asks the round table to print.",
    "_664_growth_floor": "min_fix_growth_chars (#664) is the FLOOR under the multiple, at its DEFAULTS value and the one dial in this block that LOOSENS a check: the ratio half fires only where the PR has ALSO grown past this many chars. The absolute half is untouched, so a PR of any size that grows past max_fix_growth_chars still stops.\n\nPROPORTIONALITY HAS TWO ENDS AND #492 ONLY FIXED THE TOP. Diff framing is fixed per file-hunk — diff --git, index, ---/+++, @@ and a hunk's context lines are ~430 chars before one character of the actual repair — while a multiple's allowance shrinks with the PR. Measured: on a 439-char PR the whole 3.0x allowance is 878 chars, the smallest honest single-hunk fix cost 827 of it, and 49% of the allowance went on the shape of a diff. The breakevens are 215 chars (allowance = one hunk's framing) and 413.5 (allowance = that measured minimum hunk), so below ~413 chars the ceiling cannot afford one truthful one-file fix and the dial stops meaning \"a fix pass that multiplies the diff has written a second change\". On the cycle this was measured from, the fixer named two corrections it could not pay for and the NEXT round found one of them: the ceiling caused a regression and then bought a round to rediscover it.\n\n2,000 IS A TWO-FILE FIX: ~860 of framing plus ~1,140 of content, about 17 churned lines at PR #188's measured 66 chars a line. Far below 30,000 on purpose — the floor guards the RATIO only, so a floor near the absolute ceiling would leave the multiple unable to fire first and crossed-first would have inverted. A repo that writes one at or above max_fix_growth_chars is told so in config_notes. null is the floor off and the exact pre-#664 behaviour; 0 is refused, because a floor every growth clears is the off position written in a spelling that does not say so.\n\nTHE SHAPE IS A HUMAN'S DECISION AND #664 IS LABELLED THAT WAY. The alternative is to measure both ends NET OF FRAMING, which measures the thing the dial claims to measure and needs no key at all; it is rejected because pr_chars on every baseline in flight is a RAW char count, so a framing-net numerator would decline to run on every cycle in flight and every payload behind it — #169's ships-unwired failure, and the same objection #492 used to reject a _lines dial. #551 is this question from the other end and this key does NOT settle it: what low_severity_fix_lines wants is a CEILING, min(lines, pct x the first round), because the accumulation it measures is dangerous on the SMALL PR — same question, opposite operator.",
    "_165_max_rounds": "Six, as of 2026-08-30, from two. Rich's decision on #621, per PR review (#626). Every setting this key has held is kept below, because it has now been moved in both directions on evidence and the arguments are the way back.\n\nWHY SIX. THE CAP IS A BACKSTOP AGAINST RUNNING FOREVER, NOT A CONVERGENCE MECHANISM, and one and two were both asked to be both. The metric this epic is judged on is the share of cycles ending in a CONFIDENT DRY ROUND; a cycle that ends on the cap has not produced one, it has produced a fix nobody read and a remainder handed to somebody. The evidence is lexray#1780: five rounds, to-fix counts 5 → 7 → 12 at a P2 floor, both remaining P1s caused by the fix pass before them, the PR growing from +1529/18 files to +2891/27 files, and 1,313 lines written by fix passes after round 1 of which 848 were test and doc. Nothing in that cycle was converging, and a cap of two would not have converged it — it would have shipped round 1's fix unread.\n\nWHAT NOW CARRIES THE LOAD: escalate_on.fix_injection, which says in its own words that it bites when a caller raises the cap. At two, the only round it could fire on was the round the cap was ending anyway, so what it bought was a better stop reason; at six it is the brake that decides when a cycle stops. Read _489_fix_injection below with that in mind — and read its 0.5 as UNCALIBRATED FOR THE POPULATION IT NOW COUNTS, since with observations no longer counted as outstanding work (#623) the findings it divides are a smaller and more serious set than the P3/P4-heavy ones the number was measured against. Nobody has measured where a healthy cycle sits on that population. The first cycles run under six are that measurement, and #626 is where the per-round marginal-findings count they feed belongs.\n\nWHY IT WAS CUT TO ONE on 2026-08-20: all seven panels in #165's measurement ended on the cap, and so did all four PRs open that day. A round cap that is always reached is not a safety net, it is a budget — and the findings it stops at are recorded as unactioned debt rather than fixed.\n\nWHY IT WENT BACK TO TWO on 2026-08-22: round 2 is where this repo's own evidence says the expensive defects are found — it caught the FIFO hang that round 1's fix created on PR #236, and on PR #299 five rounds produced 39 of 53 findings after round 1 introduced by the previous fix pass, round 2 being 17 of 17. A cycle that cannot re-read its own fix cannot see that at all. Three things landed on 2026-08-21 that made a second round affordable: escalate_on.premise_repeated (#84), low_severity_fix_lines (#297) and a max_fix_growth (#298) that measures the ratio it was always supposed to.\n\nTHE WAY BACK IS ONE KEY: two is the 2026-08-22 setting and one the 2026-08-20 one, with the arguments for both above. Note what one also does — it switches escalate_on.premise_repeated off, because there is no second fix pass for it to refuse — and what any cap below three does to fix_injection, new_findings_not_falling and unrefereed_fix: it returns them to annotations on a round that was ending anyway. panel.py --max-rounds still wins over this for a single run, unless the BOARD states max_rounds, in which case its value is a ceiling neither this file nor the flag may exceed (#55).",
    "_55_budget": "#55's spend ceiling. All five keys are null, meaning NO CEILING, and they are written out at that value for the same reason the dials above are: this repo is the panel's test bench and a reader should not have to open harness_rules.py to find out what governed a run.\n\nTHE FIFTH WAS SET AND REVERTED, 2026-08-30 to 2026-08-31. tokens_per_pr went to 20,000,000 on #621 beside max_rounds 6 so the later rounds could be afforded (#483, #626), and came back to null the next day. WHAT KILLED IT IS A PROPERTY OF THE BLOCK, NOT OF THE NUMBER: Budget.dormant holds only while EVERY one of these is null, and a dormant budget returns from panel_caps.check before any board call. Setting one key wakes the whole block for every repo on the fleet, and a fetch_spend that cannot answer — no board configured, a 401, a 404, or a 5s SPEND_TIMEOUT against the unbounded pr_total aggregate — then reaches _unverified(headless=True) and REFUSES before any seat runs, not overridable by --force. run-loop.sh exports HARNESS_UNATTENDED=1, so that is the autonomous loop: an unreachable board would have stopped review altogether. And it bought nothing, which is what settles it — dormant already meant no ceiling, so six rounds were affordable without the key. Set it again when the unattended path WARNS on an unverifiable spend instead of refusing, or when #483's per-round allowance exists. The argument for the number itself, below, still stands. BE CLEAR WHAT IT DOES — IT TIGHTENS, IT DOES NOT RAISE. No per-PR ceiling was in force anywhere: no fleet dial, no repo dial, and lexray's own review_panel block is empty, so the 3,000,000 that refused a round in #483 is not a current setting on anything. This is a ceiling arriving where there was none.\n\nTHE SHAPE COMPLAINT IN #483 IS UNFIXED AND THIS DOES NOT FIX IT. A per-PR ceiling binds latest and hardest on the LAST rounds of a cycle — it spends itself on rounds 1 and 2, which were happening anyway, and refuses round 3 — which is exactly the band of rounds max_rounds 6 was raised to buy, and exactly the rounds that read the fixer's own work. The fix is a per-ROUND allowance with the per-PR total derived from it (#483's proposals 1 and 2) and it is not built. So the number is chosen LARGE ENOUGH NOT TO BIND while that is outstanding: at #483's own measured 1.2M-1.7M tokens per round on four seats, six rounds is 7.2M-10.2M, and this is about twice the top of that range — the two rounds that spent 3,369,350 there fit under it nearly six times over. It is a runaway stop, not a policy about how much review a change deserves. null is the one-key way back.\n\nWHERE THE NUMBER BELONGS, AND IT IS NOT HERE. A ceiling a repo can raise from inside itself is decorative, so these five are board dials (POST /dials, human-only writes behind the Authelia edge) and the board's value beats whatever this file says. Writing one here is a self-imposed bound, honoured when the board has stated none; it is not a way to raise one the board has stated.\n\nWHAT THE UNITS ARE. Tokens are input + output — /review/stats' own billable, since cached input is a slice OF input and reasoning sits inside output for some vendors and beside it for others. #15 landed the per-reviewer capture that makes that measurable, so the ceiling is denominated in the honest unit rather than in #55's crude reviews-per-day proxy. The RUN ceilings are not that proxy's leftovers: an uninstrumented seat (antigravity) and any run recorded before v2.14 report no tokens at all, so a token-only ceiling reads an unmeasured spend as no spend — and runs_per_pr is the only one of the five that binds a caller which renumbers its rounds, since a run is a row on the board whatever it called itself.\n\n0 is a real value and means nothing may be spent; null means no ceiling. --force does not override any of them.",
    "_483_budget_round": "#483's PER-ROUND ALLOWANCE, and it is now the PRIMARY of the two per-PR ceilings. tokens_per_pr is DERIVED from it \u2014 tokens_per_round x max_rounds \u2014 rather than set beside it, and a tokens_per_pr written BELOW that total is refused by name at resolve time, before any board call, because #483's complaint is that the contradiction stays invisible until a late round is refused in front of whoever was waiting for the review.\n\nWHY THE ROUND IS THE UNIT. A round is what gets dispatched and what a seat's cost is a function of. A flat per-PR total does not bound spend per unit of work, it bounds HOW MANY UNITS OF WORK a PR is allowed \u2014 which is the decision the round logic is supposed to make on evidence \u2014 and it binds latest and hardest on the round that matters most: it is spent on rounds 1 and 2, which were happening anyway, and refuses round 3, the first round that reads the fixer's own commit and the band max_rounds 6 was raised to buy. MEASURED, over this board's own recorded history (GET /reviews?days=365&limit=500 \u2014 the params matter, limit defaults to 50 and these counts do not reproduce without them; 115 recorded review runs): of the seven PRs that reached round 3 at all, rounds 1 and 2 take a MEDIAN 57% of the PR's whole measured spend (n=7, range 0% to 89%, where the 0% is a PR whose first two rounds recorded no tokens at all). On the worst of them a flat total had 11% of itself left for the four rounds that followed.\n\nHOW IT IS ENFORCED, given that the check runs before any seat is dispatched and the round about to start has therefore spent nothing of its own to measure. The allowance is RELEASED ONE ROUND AT A TIME: at a round boundary the ceiling in force is tokens_per_round x min(rounds already recorded + 1, max_rounds), checked against pr_total.tokens. So a round that overspent its allowance is refused at the NEXT boundary \u2014 a round gone wrong, which is the meaningful refusal \u2014 while a cycle whose rounds each stayed inside the allowance can run every round the cap allows. Under a flat total the whole cycle's worth is available to round 1 in one go, which is the runaway a ceiling is actually for. Rounds are counted from the BOARD's rows (pr_total.runs, the same aggregate over the same rows as pr_total.tokens) and never from --round, which restarts at 1 under --new-cycle while pr_total has no time bound at all.\n\nNULL, LIKE ALL THE OTHERS, AND THE SIZE IS A HUMAN'S CALL RATHER THAN AN OMISSION. Two things stop a number here. First the block property recorded in _55_budget: setting ANY of these keys wakes the budget for every repo on the fleet and turns an unanswerable board into a refusal on the unattended path, which is what reverted tokens_per_pr 20,000,000 on 2026-08-31, and this key does not change that. Second, the board's own history cannot state a per-round figure (same query): 72 of its 115 recorded review runs are the fleet's ordinary four-seat shape and NOT ONE of them measured all four seats \u2014 62 measured three, five measured two, five measured none. Over the 62 that measured three, the measured \u2014 therefore undercounting \u2014 spend is median 729,755 tokens per round, p75 1,973,566, p90 4,506,368, min 185,377, max 10,049,641: a 54x spread inside one seat configuration, which makes any single value a policy choice and not a measurement. #483's own 1.2M-1.7M figure is one PR in another repo on a seat mix this fleet does not run, and is itself stated as measured over 6 of 8 reviewer runs. So the mechanism ships and the number waits for a person; #483 has the arithmetic laid out at each candidate value.",
    "_55_budget_window_hours": "What \"per day\" means, in hours, for the two rolling ceilings. A dial rather than a constant because the window and the ceiling are one decision — halving the window halves the ceiling — and a fleet that could set only one of them would be setting a number whose meaning it could not see. The per-PR ceilings have no window at all: they are about one pull request's whole cost, and a rolling window would let a PR reviewed daily for a fortnight stay under a ceiling it passed on day two.",
    "_84_escalate_on": "#78's reserved matters, of which one is built: premise_repeated (#84), left at its DEFAULTS value and written out anyway for the same reason as the eight dials above. It is the futility brake — the second time a fix pass is written against one declared premise, the cycle stops and a human answers the premise. Evaluated when a fix is PROPOSED (`panel.py --premise`), not at the end of a round, because end-of-round is one whole fix pass and one whole panel too late. The measurement was taken here: PR #299, 2026-08-21, five rounds, of which rounds 1, 2 and 3 each found the previous round's fix reopening the same hole — patched through merge parents, then same-named refs, then a purely local branch — with the premise underneath all three (that a local repository can say where a release number LANDED) named at round 3 by a human. 39 of the 53 findings after round 1 were introduced by the previous fix pass; round 2 was 17 of 17. Note what max_rounds: 1 above means for it in THIS repo: one round is one fix pass, so a premise cannot repeat unless a caller raises the cap (`--rounds N`, which is exactly what #299 did). null is the off switch; quorum_failed and judge_absent are accepted and reported as unenforced.",
    "_489_fix_injection": "#489's injection gate, the block's second implemented matter, left at its DEFAULTS value and written out anyway like the dials above — this repo is the panel's test bench and the measurement behind the number was taken here. It is the fraction of a round's NEW outstanding findings that `_provenance` attributed to the fix pass immediately before it. Above 0.5 — more than half the round's news being the fix pass's own damage — the cycle ends, with a veto line and confident false. The rationale is that from round 2 what a round reviews IS the previous round's fix, so round_stop's rule 1 is fed by the loop's own output, and a termination test fed by its own output can only end on the cap.\n\nTHE INSTRUMENT CAME FIRST AND THIS IS THE CALIBRATION ARRIVING. panel.py withheld this gate deliberately and says so in as many words: #67 asks for the instrument before the gate, and a few dozen cycles of it are what would justify wiring it to anything. The cycles: 128 of 201 new findings across the seven PRs panelled on 2026-08-16 (see _165 above); 39 of 53 after round 1 on PR #299, its round 2 being 17 of 17 (see _84_escalate_on); 64% then 87% on the cycle #489 was filed from, over a PR whose actual change was 113 lines. Every one of those is far above 0.5 and every one ran to its cap.\n\nWHAT IS STILL NOT CALIBRATED is where a HEALTHY cycle sits, which is the number that decides the false-positive rate — so read this as an EXPERIMENT with a one-key way back. What makes it affordable is that the rule can only ever turn a `go again` into a STOP: a dry round, a below-floor policy stop and a round holding an escalation all keep the reason and the confidence they earned, so no value of this can make a review look cleaner than it is. And under max_rounds 2 the only round it can fire on is the one the cap would have ended anyway, which means what it buys this repo today is a better stop reason and one more veto line rather than an earlier finish. It bites when a caller raises the cap — `--max-rounds 5`, which is exactly what #299 did.\n\nA THRESHOLD ON THIS NUMBER ERRS SAFE. _provenance documents its own bias: `introduced` requires exact membership in the fix pass's added lines and cannot see a defect the fix introduced by DELETING a guard, so the count is a floor rather than a measurement. A measured 0.64 is at least 0.64. The unattributable buckets sit in the denominator for the same reason — they depress the rate, so a round the harness could not place does not end a cycle. null (or false) is the off switch; 1 and above are REFUSED rather than clamped, because a rate is at most 1.0 and the comparison is strict, so such a value would be the brake off behind a number that reads as armed. The minimum denominator is panel_rounds.FIX_INJECTION_MIN_NEW (4) and is a constant, not a second dial: a rate over two findings is not a rate.\n\nAS OF 2026-08-30 THIS IS THE LOAD-BEARING BRAKE, and two sentences above are now out of date by design. max_rounds is 6 (see _165_max_rounds), so \"the only round it can fire on is the one the cap would have ended anyway\" no longer holds: this rung, new_findings_not_falling and unrefereed_fix are what end a cycle now, and a false positive costs a real round rather than a better stop reason. AND 0.5 IS UNCALIBRATED FOR THE POPULATION IT NOW COUNTS — with observations no longer counted as outstanding work (#623), the findings it divides are a smaller and more serious set than the P3/P4-heavy ones every number above was measured on, so neither the threshold nor its false-positive rate transfers. The first cycles run under a cap of 6 are that measurement, not a validation of it; #626 is where the per-round counts belong. null is still the one-key way back.",
    "_165_require_failing_test": "False, and it is the one key here that enforces nothing. #165 argues the evidence contract is the real termination condition \u2014 a finding ships a reproducible failing test or it is an observation \u2014 and it needs an artefact nobody emits: #92's standing decision is that a reviewer never gains an execution capability (it EMITS a test, CI or the fixer runs it) and #114 requires the test be shown RED against the unfixed code. Turning it on here would be recorded, reported and inert, and the round says so in config_notes. The key exists so the work has a home and this repo can opt in the day it is built."
  },
  "in_flight": {
    "max": null,
    "min": null,
    "_": "#337's bound on concurrent work, AT ITS DEFAULT, which is no bound at all. Written out rather than omitted for the same reason as sonarqube and preland.disabled_checks: null is also the built-in default, and a reader should not have to guess whether the absence was a decision. It is one \u2014 this repo is where the fan-out was measured (eight agents against one main on 2026-08-22: two branches minted migration 0029 independently, a third was renumbered twice mid-flight, the largest open diff went DIRTY the moment the first landed) and it is deliberately still unbounded, because the machinery ships before the number does. Set `max` here when somebody is around to watch what a throttled fleet does.",
    "_max": "The ceiling: how many units of work may be in flight in THIS repo at once, fleet-wide. The count is claims \u2014 `GET /claims/in-flight` counts live `work` claims naming an issue or a PR here, whoever holds them \u2014 and it is enforced at the checkout, by `qb-admit`, which `create-worktree` calls before it takes the claim. A full window REFUSES the checkout, the same refusal `--require-claim` already makes when the issue is taken, at the same moment, for a second reason. Admission, not queueing: the item stays on the plan, unclaimed and visibly waiting. 0 is a legal value and means admit nothing, which is a freeze rather than a typo; a value that is neither null nor a non-negative integer is reported and NOT enforced, because failing open on a malformed ceiling is the safer of the two failures for a fleet nobody is watching.",
    "_min": "The floor, and NOTHING READS IT. It exists so the planner's discretion (#232) has a configured home when it is built: a ceiling with no floor starves throughput the moment everything queued is genuinely disjoint, and `disjoint` is a judgement that needs the changed-file overlap (#101/#287) and a planner that does not exist yet. Recorded, reported, inert \u2014 the shape `review_panel.require_failing_test` already has here.",
    "_not_here": "Four things #337 asks for that this key deliberately does NOT carry yet, so nobody reads the block as more governed than it is. The ceiling is ADVISORY: `qb-admit` counts and then `create-worktree` claims, so two checkouts starting in the same second both see room and the window can be exceeded by however many raced \u2014 making it exact means moving admission into the board so the count and the insert are one transaction, which is not a change to ship alongside a bound that is off. The planner's discretion inputs \u2014 merge-conflict risk, blast radius \u2014 are absent. So is the migration slot: `kind=migration, key=<repo>` is the same claim primitive and probably wants a ceiling of 1, and whether it is a separate slot or a heavy weighting inside this window is open. And `--no-claim` remains the way to take a checkout outside the count entirely, which is consistent (outside qb is outside qb) and is warned about loudly at the checkout rather than allowed silently."
  },

  "preland": {
    "disabled_checks": [],
    "_": "Every check preland.py can detect, it runs here. Stated explicitly rather than omitted, for the same reason as sonarqube and epic.sub_pr_merge below: the default is an empty list too, and a reader should not have to guess whether the absence was a decision. It was.",
    "_the_one_worth_naming": "`review` is the check a repo would be tempted to disable, because it HOLDs when the board cannot be read at all — an unset QUARTERBACK_BASE_URL is not evidence the PR is clean, it is the absence of evidence either way. This repo IS the board, is enrolled on it, and records every panel run there, so turning it off would be turning off the only check that reads what the panel actually said. If a future box cannot reach the board, the answer is to fix the box or disable it for THAT checkout, not to commit the switch here."
  },

  "issue_pickup": {
    "enabled": false,
    "only_labels": [],
    "allowed_authors": ["prisonblues"],
    "require_human_triage": true,
    "skip_labels": ["needs-human/*"],
    "skip_when_unlabelled": true,
    "_": "What a loop may pick up of its OWN accord (#85, #86). Every value here is the default; they are written out rather than omitted because this is the block a reader will come to when they want to know whether anything on this repo can start work unprompted, and an empty block answers that with silence. The answer is no: `enabled` is false, and nothing turns it on implicitly. NOTE, since #277: this block governs what a loop ALREADY RUNNING may take of its own accord, and it is not the whole answer to \"can anything start work here unprompted\". Whether a session may be STARTED at all is `qb-start`, and that switch is per MACHINE (`programs.quarterback-harness.spawn.enable`) rather than per repo \u2014 deliberately, because it is a remote-execution primitive on the box rather than a policy about this codebase. It ships off too, and it is not a way round this gate: a spawned session is told a command and a number by whoever ran qb-start, and anything it then picks up on its own account comes back through here. Since #63 this block has a consumer: `issue_watch.py` reads a repo's open issues and says what each one is waiting on. Its SURVEY half needs no permission and runs either way \u2014 it chooses no work \u2014 but the action it names is gated here, and with this block as shipped that action is `none` for every issue.",
    "_allowed_authors": "Stated ahead of the gate it guards, on purpose. This repo is PUBLIC and anyone can open an issue; under #63's watcher that text becomes the instructions for an agent with a full shell on zeus. The mitigation has to be an allowlist and not a filter — a filter is a list of the phrasings somebody already thought of, and the attack is the one nobody thought of. Writing the allowlist now means turning the watcher on later is one switch rather than one switch plus a security decision made in the same hurried minute.",
    "_only_labels_is_still_empty": "Deliberately, and it is the second lock. Even with `enabled` flipped, nothing qualifies until this repo says what a pickup-able issue looks like. Turning the gate on is one decision; saying what may come through it is another.",
    "_require_human_triage": "The load-bearing line. An allowlist of labels authorises nothing if the agent can apply the labels — #78's `judge_model` problem one level out — so appetite.py reads the issue's label EVENTS and asks who applied it. NOTE, because it decides whether this setting is usable at all: nothing in harness/ writes labels today (`gh issue edit --add-label` appears nowhere), so the only thing that can open this gate is a person in the GitHub UI or typing `gh` by hand. That is the intended opener and not an oversight — but if a labelling agent is ever added, it MUST be named in `agent_actors` on the same commit, or this setting silently becomes decorative.",
    "_skip_labels": "#279's closed vocabulary (`needs-human/decision|taste|ui|environment|auth|other`), matched as a glob so the `other` escape hatch can grow the vocabulary a word without this list going stale by letting the new class through. #86 proposed design/ui/decision-owed/needs-scoping, written when this repo had no labels at all; these six exist and are already produced, and two vocabularies for one idea is the drift #65 has been paid for once."
  },

  "issue_filing": {
    "max_per_run": 1,
    "require_dedup_check": true,
    "unattended": false,
    "_": "How much a loop may FILE — the risk in the other direction (#85). Nine issues in one day, every one a response to something real, which is what makes it a risk rather than a bug: a loop applying the same standard with no ceiling produces a backlog nobody reads.",
    "_unattended": "Restates #40's standing decision as config, so a repo can relax it deliberately rather than by accident. An unattended run may still REPORT what it would have filed; that half was never the problem.",
    "_require_dedup_check": "Matters more than it looks. It is a search over the title's own words rather than an exact match, because the duplicate that matters is the one somebody phrased differently — an exact-title check would pass every time and read as a working gate."
  },

  "epic": {
    "sub_pr_merge": "gate",
    "_": "Whether the epic driver merges a reviewed sub-PR into the epic branch by itself, or holds each at a human merge. Stated explicitly for the same reason as sonarqube above — the default is `gate` too, and a reader should not have to guess whether that was a decision. It was.",
    "_why_off": "This is the switch that decides whether the merge gate merges anything, and that gate has now been wrong on its first attempt three rounds running: it read a zero exit as a review, then a push as a review, then the existence of a --json-file payload as a review. panel.py defeats all three — it exits 0, and writes a payload, on a title-pattern skip. The current fix reads the panel's own `reviewed`/`skip_reason`/`reviewers_ran` instead, which has no fourth proxy to get wrong; but 'we got it right this time' is what the previous three said, and defaulting closed makes a fourth mistake cost a printed line rather than an unreviewed merge.",
    "_when_to_flip": "When #78 lands `escalate_on` and `quorum` beside it, so the gate is governed rather than merely correct — and ideally when #77 can show that a confirmed finding survived contact with the code. Flipping it is one line and needs no code change."
  }
}
